1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:04,910
Greetings, aliens and earthlings! This is
part 2 of our mini-series about the shape

2
00:00:04,910 --> 00:00:06,240
of the universe.

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:09,830
If you didn't watch part 1, I suggest you
watch it first.

4
00:00:09,830 --> 00:00:12,030
Today we will speak about curvature of space.

5
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:47,670
To be clear, we will talk about curvature
of space, not about curvature of spacetime,

6
00:00:47,670 --> 00:00:50,340
as examined by general relativity.

7
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:51,430
What is curvature?

8
00:00:51,430 --> 00:00:55,370
For a surface, curvature means that the surface
cannot be flattened without stretching or

9
00:00:55,370 --> 00:00:56,460
squeezing it.

10
00:00:56,460 --> 00:01:00,920
A bend piece of paper can be flattened, but
try flattening a sphere cap, or a saddle surface:

11
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:02,539
It won't work.

12
00:01:02,539 --> 00:01:03,769
That's because of the curvature.

13
00:01:03,769 --> 00:01:07,940
A sphere is said to have positive curvature,
aka spherical curvature.

14
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:11,720
A saddle surface has negative curvature, aka
hyperbolic curvature.

15
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:15,050
A surface can be differently curved at different
locations.

16
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:19,900
A banana for example is positively curved
on one side, and negatively on the other side.

17
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:24,830
Now, the difference is easily visible "from
the outside", but how to explain it to flatsnails

18
00:01:24,830 --> 00:01:26,350
inside the surface?

19
00:01:26,350 --> 00:01:29,440
Well, you could try to explain it with triangles.

20
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:35,560
On a flat plane, the angles of a triangle
sum up to 180°, no matter it's size.

21
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:39,690
Why is this so? Well, imagine a flatsnail
following the border.

22
00:01:39,690 --> 00:01:44,220
Be patient, it's a snail.

23
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:46,390
This might take a while.

24
00:01:46,390 --> 00:01:49,350
You know what? Forget about the flatsnail.

25
00:01:49,350 --> 00:01:54,860
Let's take a shark! On a lightcycle! With
a fricking laser attached to his head! Now,

26
00:01:54,860 --> 00:01:55,920
that's better.

27
00:01:55,920 --> 00:02:01,650
Each time the shark takes a corner, the laser
goes "woosh".

28
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:06,040
And afterwards, he has made a full turn - so,
the sums of the "wooshs" sum up to a full

29
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:07,260
turn.

30
00:02:07,260 --> 00:02:11,200
But each "woosh" sums up with one angle to
180°.

31
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:15,120
From this you can easily calculate that the
sum of the angles is 180°.

32
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:18,390
Now, let's look what happens on a sphere.

33
00:02:18,390 --> 00:02:25,030
This triangle, for example, has three 90°
angles. 270° in total.

34
00:02:25,030 --> 00:02:27,410
Where do the additional 90° come from?

35
00:02:27,410 --> 00:02:28,820
The answer: Curvature.

36
00:02:28,820 --> 00:02:33,200
The sphere has positive curvature, which somehow
inflates the angles.

37
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:37,770
Small triangles behave almost as on a plane,
but the bigger they are, the more the angles

38
00:02:37,770 --> 00:02:38,470
grow.

39
00:02:38,470 --> 00:02:42,330
Think of curvature as overpressure: It gives
the triangles a cussion form and makes the

40
00:02:42,330 --> 00:02:43,900
angles less pointy.

41
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:48,960
But what about a negatively curved surface
- a saddle surface? Here, the opposite happens:

42
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:51,800
The angles are inferior to what you might
expect.

43
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:55,340
Think of negative curvature as underpressure
which gives the triangles an evacuated shape

44
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:57,220
and makes corners pointier.

45
00:02:57,220 --> 00:03:02,290
By the way, the same happens to arbitrary
polygons: Negative curvature sucks them out,

46
00:03:02,290 --> 00:03:03,650
positive pumps them up.

47
00:03:03,650 --> 00:03:07,400
But where does this strange behaviour come
from? You might get an idea when you try to

48
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:10,840
put a round patch of fabric onto a positively
curved surface.

49
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:14,310
You'll notice that near the border, there
is too much fabric - you have to hide fabric

50
00:03:14,310 --> 00:03:16,750
triangles by folding and sewing.

51
00:03:16,750 --> 00:03:19,460
Tailors call the hidden triangles "darts".

52
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:23,600
So, what happens here is that in the case
of positive curvature, the border of disks

53
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:25,670
is shorter than you might expect.

54
00:03:25,670 --> 00:03:29,990
In other words, the outskirts of any region
are smaller than you might expect.

55
00:03:29,990 --> 00:03:33,069
There is surface that has been "sewn away",
so to say.

56
00:03:33,069 --> 00:03:37,130
In consequence, when you connect three or
more points with shortest lines, the lines

57
00:03:37,130 --> 00:03:40,930
have the tendency to bulge outwards, in order
to benefit from the shorter distances in the

58
00:03:40,930 --> 00:03:42,400
outskirts.

59
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:46,140
It's like intercontinental flights on Earth:
The flight paths have the tendency to bulge

60
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:51,660
towards the poles, because near a pole, the
distance between lines of longitude is shorter.

61
00:03:51,660 --> 00:03:55,770
That's the reason why polygons have a cussion
form on positively curved surfaces.

62
00:03:55,770 --> 00:03:59,810
Another thing that happens on a positively
curved surface is that parallels converge.

63
00:03:59,810 --> 00:04:03,910
The reason is, so to speak, the fabric between
the parallels is "sewn away".

64
00:04:03,910 --> 00:04:08,459
Now, what about negatively curved surfaces?
Let's try to put a round patch of fabric onto

65
00:04:08,459 --> 00:04:10,030
a saddle surface.

66
00:04:10,030 --> 00:04:14,950
We notice that it doesn't work, unless we
tear the border apart, or insert fabric triangles

67
00:04:14,950 --> 00:04:16,510
to adapt to the shape.

68
00:04:16,510 --> 00:04:21,180
So, in this case, we have the inverse effect:
Far from the center we have more surface than

69
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:22,690
you might expect.

70
00:04:22,690 --> 00:04:27,180
In consequence, shortest connections between
corners avoid the outskirts and take a shortcut

71
00:04:27,180 --> 00:04:29,520
through the center where distances are smaller.

72
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:33,460
That's why the edges bulge inwards, and the
corners are pointier than on a plane.

73
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:37,650
For example, if earth were a hyperboloid,
you would see flight paths bending towards

74
00:04:37,650 --> 00:04:40,510
the equator, because there distances are shorter.

75
00:04:40,510 --> 00:04:45,740
What happens to parallel lines on a negatively
curved surface? Well, they diverge.

76
00:04:45,740 --> 00:04:49,860
That happens, so to speak, because of the
additional fabric inserted between them.

77
00:04:49,860 --> 00:04:54,380
Now, can we do the same in three-dimensional
space? Of course we can.

78
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:57,669
The three-dimensional equivalent to a round
patch is a spherical ball.

79
00:04:57,669 --> 00:05:01,889
Now, in flat space, a ball behaves exactly
how it should.

80
00:05:01,889 --> 00:05:05,510
But in a positively curved universe, the surface
of a big ball is smaller.

81
00:05:05,510 --> 00:05:10,320
In other words, outer space is smaller than
you think - as if someone had stolen whole

82
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:15,010
wedges of space and bridged the rest with
portals, hoping nobody would notice.

83
00:05:15,010 --> 00:05:19,360
In a negatively curved universe, the surface
of a ball is bigger, there is more outer space

84
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:20,889
than you might think.

85
00:05:20,889 --> 00:05:23,610
As if someone had inserted additional space.

86
00:05:23,610 --> 00:05:27,800
The three-dimensional analogon to polygons
are polyhedra - and they react to curvature

87
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:29,150
in the same way.

88
00:05:29,150 --> 00:05:33,210
When you take a polyhedron and put it into
positively curved space, the edges and faces

89
00:05:33,210 --> 00:05:36,350
bulge outwards to benefit from the shrunken
outer space.

90
00:05:36,350 --> 00:05:39,270
In negatively curved space, the opposite happens.

91
00:05:39,270 --> 00:05:44,530
Here, outer space is spacier than you think,
so the edges and faces bulge inwards to awoid

92
00:05:44,530 --> 00:05:46,710
those long outer-space distances.

93
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:50,910
You may think of positive curvature as overpressure
which blows up polygons to a cussion form

94
00:05:50,910 --> 00:05:53,560
and makes corners more obtuse.

95
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:56,770
Negative curvature is like underpressure which
gives the polygon an evacuated shape with

96
00:05:56,770 --> 00:05:58,130
pointy corners.

97
00:05:58,130 --> 00:06:02,790
But is our universe flat or curved? To find
that out, we would have to measure really,

98
00:06:02,790 --> 00:06:06,419
really big triangles or polyhedra, and that's
not easy.

99
00:06:06,419 --> 00:06:08,720
We will come to this point next episode.

100
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:13,520
Our goal today is to understand what curvature
is, and to see some examples.

101
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:15,770
The universe could even have mixed curvature.

102
00:06:15,770 --> 00:06:20,690
Remember the banana? It's curvature is positive
on one side, negative on the other one.

103
00:06:20,690 --> 00:06:23,310
Couldn't the universe be the same - some kind
of hyperbanana?

104
00:06:23,310 --> 00:06:25,460
Well, yes and no.

105
00:06:25,460 --> 00:06:29,600
It could be, but that would violate something
called the "cosmological principle".

106
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:33,919
That's the assumption that on a large scale,
the universe looks everywhere the same.

107
00:06:33,919 --> 00:06:38,960
It's not a patchwork of different zones with
different properties, shapes and laws of physics.

108
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:43,760
This excludes "mixed curvature", just as it
excludes zones with different physical constants,

109
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:45,710
and just as it excludes an edge.

110
00:06:45,710 --> 00:06:49,449
This assumption is a consequence of what earthlings
call "Ockham's Razor", the principle that

111
00:06:49,449 --> 00:06:54,750
simple hypotheses are better than complicated
ones, if both are equally supported by evidence.

112
00:06:54,750 --> 00:06:58,570
Think of Ockham's Razor as a proven search
order for hypotheses : It's useful to check

113
00:06:58,570 --> 00:07:00,860
the simplest hypotheses first.

114
00:07:00,860 --> 00:07:05,400
For example, when I see a crop circle, I could
hypothesize a conspiracy of local farm boys

115
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:10,639
who spent a whole night in a field, trampling
crops down with primitive earthling tools.

116
00:07:10,639 --> 00:07:14,259
But a much simpler hypothesis would be a fellow
alien with a cropwriter who wanted to contact

117
00:07:14,259 --> 00:07:15,520
a ship in orbit.

118
00:07:15,520 --> 00:07:17,620
Hitchhikers do this all the time.

119
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:21,800
So, there is no need for a conspiracy theory
if there is a simpler explanation.

120
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:27,610
So, from now on, let's assume that the universe
is either flat, or entirely positively curved,

121
00:07:27,610 --> 00:07:29,330
or entirely negatively curved.

122
00:07:29,330 --> 00:07:31,460
So, no hyperbanana.

123
00:07:31,460 --> 00:07:34,380
The simplest positively curved model is the
hypersphere.

124
00:07:34,380 --> 00:07:38,470
Before we can understand the hypersphere,
let's try to explain to Flatsnails how you

125
00:07:38,470 --> 00:07:40,370
can construct a sphere.

126
00:07:40,370 --> 00:07:41,680
How would you do that?

127
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:45,259
Maybe we could start with two squares, one
per hemisphere.

128
00:07:45,259 --> 00:07:49,169
We pump them up with curvature until the 90°
angles are smoothed out.

129
00:07:49,169 --> 00:07:53,289
Now, each part has a smooth, straight border
without corners.

130
00:07:53,289 --> 00:07:56,850
We might call this a zero-gon, but the usual
term is "equator".

131
00:07:56,850 --> 00:08:00,360
Then we glue the two together along the equator,
with portals.

132
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:03,550
That's probably where one of the Flatsnails
would ask why we need the curvature.

133
00:08:03,550 --> 00:08:08,610
Can't we simply take two squares, portalize
them together, and it's done? Wouldn't that

134
00:08:08,610 --> 00:08:09,240
be much easier?

135
00:08:09,240 --> 00:08:12,210
Easier maybe, but there is a problem.

136
00:08:12,210 --> 00:08:17,400
See, dear flatsnails, each corner point is
only adjacent to two square corners.

137
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:20,810
But you can't glue too 90° corners together
in a smooth manner.

138
00:08:20,810 --> 00:08:26,320
So, you have to use curvature to pump the
angles up to 180° to make a smooth gluing.

139
00:08:26,320 --> 00:08:30,310
The two "pumped up" squares would be somewhat
like a disk and somewhat like a square: They

140
00:08:30,310 --> 00:08:34,139
would have no corners, like a disk, and have
straight edges, like a square .

141
00:08:34,139 --> 00:08:37,990
"Straight" means straight for creatures inside
the surface.

142
00:08:37,990 --> 00:08:41,309
That would probably blow the mind of some
flatsnails.

143
00:08:41,309 --> 00:08:45,290
But for you, it would be easy because you
could secretly think of hemispheres.

144
00:08:45,290 --> 00:08:50,619
But now, we can do the same thing in three-dimensional
space: We take two cubes, pump them up with

145
00:08:50,619 --> 00:08:55,819
positive curvature to flatten out the corners
and edges, and glue them together with portals.

146
00:08:55,819 --> 00:09:00,139
Those pumped up cubes would be somewhat like
cubes and somewhat like spheres: Edges and

147
00:09:00,139 --> 00:09:03,050
corners smoothed out, but flat sides.

148
00:09:03,050 --> 00:09:05,149
The result is called a hypersphere.

149
00:09:05,149 --> 00:09:09,369
In the last episode we have seen that when
we take one cube and glue opposing faces together,

150
00:09:09,369 --> 00:09:11,019
we get a hypertorus.

151
00:09:11,019 --> 00:09:15,990
Why do we need positive curvature for the
hypersphere, but not for the hypertorus? Well,

152
00:09:15,990 --> 00:09:17,319
because of the corners.

153
00:09:17,319 --> 00:09:23,249
Look, in the hypertorus, this corner is identical
with this one, and this one, and - actually,

154
00:09:23,249 --> 00:09:25,179
all corners are one and the same.

155
00:09:25,179 --> 00:09:29,899
So, around this corner, we have eight parts
of the space matching perfectly together - no

156
00:09:29,899 --> 00:09:31,569
need for curvature.

157
00:09:31,569 --> 00:09:36,240
On the other hand, let's look what happens
when we glue two cubes together to a hypersphere,

158
00:09:36,240 --> 00:09:37,769
but forget the curvature.

159
00:09:37,769 --> 00:09:41,470
This corner is identical with this one, and...
that's it.

160
00:09:41,470 --> 00:09:46,639
So, the vicinity of this corner are two parts
of the two cubes - that doesn't match together.

161
00:09:46,639 --> 00:09:51,009
We have to blow up the cubes such that the
corners get smoothed out - then the gluing

162
00:09:51,009 --> 00:09:51,959
works.

163
00:09:51,959 --> 00:09:57,259
As mentioned before, the surfaces of the hemispheres
are flat - they only look curved in our 3D

164
00:09:57,259 --> 00:10:01,170
representation, because we can't display the
curvature of the space inside.

165
00:10:01,170 --> 00:10:03,670
That's why we can glue the two balls together.

166
00:10:03,670 --> 00:10:07,639
That gluing surface is, so to say, the equator
of the hypersphere.

167
00:10:07,639 --> 00:10:11,160
Let's make a journey through a hypersphere
universe at super-light speed.

168
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:15,399
Actually, there is only one thing capable
of travelling faster than light without warping

169
00:10:15,399 --> 00:10:16,860
space: A rumor.

170
00:10:16,860 --> 00:10:22,139
So, let's invent a rumor - say, the rumor
that Youtube celebrity C.G.P. Grey is actually

171
00:10:22,139 --> 00:10:23,989
a superintelligent stick figure.

172
00:10:23,989 --> 00:10:27,350
The rumor bubble expands from earth at super-light
speed.

173
00:10:27,350 --> 00:10:32,089
First, the bubble surface grows like you would
expect, but after a while, it grows slower

174
00:10:32,089 --> 00:10:34,600
and becomes less and less convex.

175
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:39,259
Eventually, the surface flips and becomes
concave - we have crossed the cosmic equator.

176
00:10:39,259 --> 00:10:41,730
The bubble is flipped inside out and shrinks
again.

177
00:10:41,730 --> 00:10:46,439
Eventually, it becomes smaller and smaller
until it vanishes in one point - the anti-podes

178
00:10:46,439 --> 00:10:47,899
of our starting point.

179
00:10:47,899 --> 00:10:51,970
So, we have constructed our first positively
curved space: The hypersphere.

180
00:10:51,970 --> 00:10:56,110
But this is far from being the only curved
model.

181
00:10:56,110 --> 00:10:59,949
In the last episode, I mentionned that when
you take a square and add two pairs of flipped

182
00:10:59,949 --> 00:11:04,220
portals such that opposing points are glued
together, you get something called projective

183
00:11:04,220 --> 00:11:05,300
plane.

184
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:08,610
What I didn't mention was that like for the
sphere, you need curvature to have smooth

185
00:11:08,610 --> 00:11:10,259
gluing at the corners.

186
00:11:10,259 --> 00:11:14,319
The projective plane is a strange and interesting
place where parallels intersect in infinity

187
00:11:14,319 --> 00:11:17,170
and all conic sections are basically the same.

188
00:11:17,170 --> 00:11:21,410
Also, it's non-orientable because it contains
a moebius strip.

189
00:11:21,410 --> 00:11:23,899
Maybe I'll make a video about this one day.

190
00:11:23,899 --> 00:11:25,749
But not anytime soon.

191
00:11:25,749 --> 00:11:28,379
You can do the same thing in three dimensions.

192
00:11:28,379 --> 00:11:32,920
You take one cube, pump it up with positive
curvature like you did for the hypersphere,

193
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:36,350
and then add portals to glue every point to
its antipodal point.

194
00:11:36,350 --> 00:11:39,499
The result is what topologists call "projective
space".

195
00:11:39,499 --> 00:11:42,739
Unlike it's two-dimensional counterpart, it's
orientable.

196
00:11:42,739 --> 00:11:45,720
Now, you don't need to start with a cube.

197
00:11:45,720 --> 00:11:52,720
Take, fro example, a dodekahedron and glue
opposing faces together.

198
00:11:53,110 --> 00:11:57,360
Opposing pentagons are oriented differently,
so you have to rotate them in some way - let's

199
00:11:57,360 --> 00:12:00,259
say, a 180° turn.

200
00:12:00,259 --> 00:12:05,089
What space is this? Well, when we pump the
result a little up, we get again the hyper-hemisphere

201
00:12:05,089 --> 00:12:07,029
with opposite points glued together.

202
00:12:07,029 --> 00:12:11,230
So, this is just another way of constructing
the projective space.

203
00:12:11,230 --> 00:12:16,929
But what if we do only a 36° turn for each
pair? We get a far more interesting space.

204
00:12:16,929 --> 00:12:20,420
Now, each corner corresponds to 3 other corners.

205
00:12:20,420 --> 00:12:24,189
When we look what happens around one corner,
we will notice that the puzzle almost matches

206
00:12:24,189 --> 00:12:26,809
together, the corners are only a bit to pointy.

207
00:12:26,809 --> 00:12:31,489
So, we have to add just a little bit of positive
curvature, to close the gaps.

208
00:12:31,489 --> 00:12:35,699
This positively curved space is called the
Poincaré dodekahedral space.

209
00:12:35,699 --> 00:12:40,569
It has been proposed in 2003 as possible shape
of the universe by an earthling called Jean-Pierre

210
00:12:40,569 --> 00:12:41,610
Luminet.

211
00:12:41,610 --> 00:12:44,470
We will come back to this in Episode 3.

212
00:12:44,470 --> 00:12:49,499
What happens when we turn the portals not
by a 1/10 turn, but by a 3/10 turn? Well,

213
00:12:49,499 --> 00:12:55,610
that changes quite a bit: Now, all corners
come together to a single point! In consequence,

214
00:12:55,610 --> 00:12:58,290
the environment of this point becomes quite
crowdy, we must

215
00:12:58,290 --> 00:13:01,449
drastically reduce the corner angles to make
place for everybody.

216
00:13:01,449 --> 00:13:06,170
In other words, we need a lot of negative
curvature to make the corners match.

217
00:13:06,170 --> 00:13:10,709
This is called the Seifert-Weber space, and
it's an example for a negatively curved finite

218
00:13:10,709 --> 00:13:11,290
model.

219
00:13:11,290 --> 00:13:14,720
Another example for a negatively curved model
would be the Picard horn.

220
00:13:14,720 --> 00:13:18,670
It's difficult to explain without refering
to higher mathematics, so I'll only sketch

221
00:13:18,670 --> 00:13:22,970
the construction of its two-dimensional analogon:
That would be a horn, where opposing points

222
00:13:22,970 --> 00:13:25,410
of the edge are glued together - when you
leave here, you

223
00:13:25,410 --> 00:13:25,749
enter here.

224
00:13:25,749 --> 00:13:31,350
The pointy end of the horn is infinitely long,
but so narrow that the volume is finite.

225
00:13:31,350 --> 00:13:35,369
In this video, we have seen three positively
curved and two negatively curved models for

226
00:13:35,369 --> 00:13:37,389
a finite universe without edge.

227
00:13:37,389 --> 00:13:41,439
Of course, I never said that the universe
is necessarily finite, I only said it's a

228
00:13:41,439 --> 00:13:44,199
possibility - it could very well be infinite.

229
00:13:44,199 --> 00:13:47,660
There are many other models, finite ones and
infinite ones.

230
00:13:47,660 --> 00:13:49,649
But I think we had enough for today.

231
00:13:49,649 --> 00:13:53,359
The objective of this episode wasn't to provide
an exhaustive list of models, but to give

232
00:13:53,359 --> 00:13:56,629
you an idea of what "curved space" actually
means.

233
00:13:56,629 --> 00:14:01,189
In the third and last episode, we will speak
about the expansion of the universe, and discuss

234
00:14:01,189 --> 00:14:05,139
hypotheses and evidence concerning the actual
shape of the universe.

235
00:14:05,139 --> 00:14:08,720
One personal note: I am more of a mathematician
than of a physicist.

236
00:14:08,720 --> 00:14:12,239
So, I can't promise I won't make any errors
in physics.

237
00:14:12,239 --> 00:14:18,399
To give an example, last time I told you that
an Alice universe would turn matter into antimatter

238
00:14:18,399 --> 00:14:22,079
- but as one viewer noted, things might not
be as simple as that.

239
00:14:22,079 --> 00:14:26,410
By the way, this comment wasn't on YouTube,
but on the site Universe Today which posted

240
00:14:26,410 --> 00:14:28,540
an article on the first episode.

241
00:14:28,540 --> 00:14:33,089
This article brought me a lot of views, thanks
for that! If you like astronomy, you will

242
00:14:33,089 --> 00:14:35,790
probably like this site - give it a try!

243
00:14:35,790 --> 00:14:39,989
As for my next videos: When I asked last time
whether you would prefer me to alternate with

244
00:14:39,989 --> 00:14:44,720
"Earthlings 101" or finish this series first,
about two thirds of you voted for finishing

245
00:14:44,720 --> 00:14:47,679
this series, and one third for alternating.

246
00:14:47,679 --> 00:14:49,989
That's why you are watching this video today.

247
00:14:49,989 --> 00:14:54,279
But that's also why I've decided to chose
a hybrid solution: After this video, I will

248
00:14:54,279 --> 00:14:59,019
programm the next episode of Earthlings 101,
and afterwards finish this series.

249
00:14:59,019 --> 00:15:03,769
This way I don't scare off viewers who don't
care at all about cosmotopology.

250
00:15:03,769 --> 00:15:04,829
That's all for today.

251
00:15:04,829 --> 00:15:09,689
Like, subscribe, tell your mum, and as always:
Don't forget to be alien!

